const schema = JSON.stringify([faqSchema, howToSchema, breadcrumbSchema]);
---

<StatePage title={title} description={description} canonical="https://mechanicslienform.com/mechanics-lien/arizona/" schema={schema}>
  <!-- Breadcrumb nav -->
  <nav class="text-sm text-gray-500 mb-6">
    <a href="/" class="hover:text-navy-700">Home</a> ›
    <a href="/mechanics-lien/" class="hover:text-navy-700">Mechanics Lien</a> ›
    <span class="text-gray-900">Arizona</span>
  </nav>

  <!-- Hero -->
  <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 py-8">
    <h1 class="text-4xl font-bold text-navy-900 mb-4">Arizona Mechanics Lien: 20-Day Notice and 120/60-Day Deadlines</h1>

    <!-- Featured snippet answer -->
    <div class="bg-navy-50 border-l-4 border-navy-600 p-4 mb-8 rounded-r-lg">
      <p class="text-navy-800 font-medium">Serve Arizona's preliminary notice within 20 days after first furnishing for full protection. Record the lien within 120 days after statutory completion — or 60 days after a recorded Notice of Completion — serve the owner within a reasonable time, and sue within six months.</p>
    </div>

    <!-- Arizona-specific deadline caveat -->
    <div class="bg-yellow-50 border border-yellow-300 p-4 rounded-lg mb-4">
      <strong>⚠️ Arizona's deadline does not run from your last furnishing date.</strong> Unlike most states, Arizona's 120-day (or 60-day, if a Notice of Completion is recorded) period runs from statutory <em>completion</em> of the improvement — not from when you last furnished labor or materials. Because of this, the tool below cannot show an automatic countdown for Arizona; verify your applicable date against county records before relying on any deadline.
    </div>

    <LienFormApp
      client:load
      defaultState="arizona"
      documentType="mechanics-lien"
      productName="Arizona Mechanics Lien Bundle"
    />

    <!-- SEO content -->
    <div class="prose prose-lg max-w-none">

      <p>An Arizona mechanics lien generally requires two timed documents: a <strong>preliminary 20-day notice</strong> near the start of furnishing and a recorded <strong>Notice and Claim of Lien</strong> after nonpayment. The lien is ordinarily recorded within <strong>120 days after completion of the improvement</strong>, but a recorded Notice of Completion shortens the period to <strong>60 days after that notice is recorded</strong>. The claimant must then serve a copy on the owner within a reasonable time and generally file a foreclosure action within <strong>six months after recording</strong>.</p>

      <p>Project completion drives Arizona's lien clock. A claimant who counts 120 days from its own last invoice may use the wrong starting date.</p>

      <p>For the underlying document fields and filing preparation, use the <a href="/mechanics-lien/form/">mechanics lien form guide</a>.</p>

      <h2>Arizona mechanics lien timeline</h2>

      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Step</th><th>General timing</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Preliminary 20-day notice</td><td>No later than 20 days after first furnishing for full protection</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Notice and Claim of Lien</td><td>120 days after statutory completion, or 60 days after a Notice of Completion is recorded</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Serve recorded lien on owner</td><td>Within a reasonable time after recording</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Foreclosure action</td><td>Within 6 months after recording</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <p>Contract language, credit terms, owner-occupant rules, licensing, and project type can change the analysis.</p>

      <h2>Who needs an Arizona preliminary 20-day notice?</h2>

      <p>With limited exceptions, a person furnishing labor, professional services, materials, machinery, fixtures, or tools must serve the statutory preliminary notice as a prerequisite to lien rights. A person performing actual labor for wages is treated differently under the statute.</p>

      <p>The notice generally goes to:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Owner or reputed owner</li>
        <li>Original contractor or reputed contractor</li>
        <li>Construction lender or reputed construction lender, if any</li>
        <li>Person who contracted for the claimant's work or materials</li>
      </ul>

      <p>The notice states the parties, project, general description of furnishing, estimated total price, and statutory warning. Only the prescribed content performs this job; an ordinary collection letter leaves the statutory notice requirement unmet. A separate <a href="/notice-of-intent-to-lien/">notice of intent to lien</a> can create collection pressure, but it serves a different purpose.</p>

      <h2>What if the preliminary notice is late?</h2>

      <p>A late Arizona 20-day notice generally protects labor, services, materials, machinery, fixtures, or tools furnished during the <strong>20 days before service and thereafter</strong>. Earlier furnishing falls outside that lookback.</p>

      <h3>Example</h3>

      <p>A supplier first delivers on April 1 but serves the notice on April 30. The late notice generally reaches back 20 days from service — not to April 1 — so early deliveries may be unprotected. The claimant should calculate the protected amount from delivery-level records rather than placing the full account balance in the lien.</p>

      <p>If the actual estimated total price later exceeds the amount in a prior notice by <strong>30% or more</strong>, A.R.S. § 33-992.01(H) requires a supplemental notice. Monitor approved changes and revised purchase orders against the noticed estimate.</p>

      <h2>What counts as completion in Arizona?</h2>

      <p>Arizona Revised Statutes § 33-993 defines completion using specific events. Depending on the project, completion can include:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Thirty days after final inspection and written final acceptance by the governmental body that issued the permit</li>
        <li>Sixty consecutive days of cessation of labor, subject to statutory exceptions</li>
        <li>If no permit or final acceptance applies, the last date labor, materials, fixtures, or tools were furnished to the property</li>
      </ul>

      <p>An owner may record a Notice of Completion, shortening the lien-recording period from 120 days after completion to 60 days after the notice is recorded. Claimants should monitor county records because owner notification alone is an unreliable deadline system.</p>

      <h2>What the Arizona Notice and Claim of Lien requires</h2>

      <p>The recorded claim generally includes:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Legal description of the property</li>
        <li>Owner or reputed owner</li>
        <li>Claimant and hiring party</li>
        <li>Statement of the claimant's demand after deducting offsets and credits</li>
        <li>Dates of first and last furnishing</li>
        <li>Date of project completion</li>
        <li>Date the preliminary 20-day notice was given</li>
        <li>Signature and verification</li>
      </ul>

      <p>Attach a copy of the preliminary 20-day notice and the statutory proof of mailing. An otherwise polished claim can fail if required attachments are missing.</p>

      <h2>How to file an Arizona mechanics lien</h2>

      <ol>
        <li><strong>Confirm eligibility and licensing.</strong> Arizona contractor licensing issues can affect recovery and lien rights.</li>
        <li><strong>Audit the preliminary notice.</strong> Identify recipients, service date, estimated price, and any needed supplement.</li>
        <li><strong>Determine statutory completion.</strong> Obtain permit/final inspection records and search for a recorded Notice of Completion.</li>
        <li><strong>Calculate both possible deadlines.</strong> Track 120 days after completion and 60 days after recorded Notice of Completion; use the earlier applicable date.</li>
        <li><strong>Verify ownership and legal description.</strong> Use current county records.</li>
        <li><strong>Calculate the protected amount.</strong> Apply payments, credits, and any late-notice lookback limitation.</li>
        <li><strong>Prepare and verify the claim.</strong> Include statutory contents and attachments.</li>
        <li><strong>Record with the county recorder.</strong> File in the county where the property is located.</li>
        <li><strong>Serve the owner within a reasonable time.</strong> Preserve proof and serve promptly. In <em>Wang Electric, Inc. v. Smoke Tree Resort, LLC</em>, an Arizona appellate court declined to rule that service roughly three months after recording was unreasonable on the record before it. The same decision explained that <em>Old Adobe Office Properties, Ltd. v. Gin</em> found service near the end of the six-month lien period unreasonable on its facts. Neither decision creates a safe waiting period.</li>
        <li><strong>Calendar six months to foreclose.</strong> Engage counsel well before expiration.</li>
      </ol>

      <h2>Owner-occupied dwellings require special attention</h2>

      <p>Arizona limits lien rights against certain owner-occupied dwellings. A party who has no direct contract with the owner-occupant may not have the same lien remedy available on a dwelling as on a commercial project or non-owner-occupied residence. Confirm the property type, occupancy, and direct contractual relationship before threatening or recording a claim.</p>

      <p>Resolve this threshold issue before anyone prepares or records the document; correct margins cannot create a lien right the statute withholds.</p>

      <h2>Arizona and California use different lien clocks</h2>

      <p>Both states use a 20-day preliminary-notice concept, but the later filing analysis differs. Arizona generally measures from statutory <strong>project completion</strong> and may shorten the period through a recorded Notice of Completion. California deadlines depend on claimant role, project completion or cessation, and recorded completion or cessation notices. Use the <a href="/mechanics-lien/california/">California mechanics lien guide</a> for a California property rather than transferring Arizona's 120/60-day framework across the state line.</p>

      <h2>Common Arizona lien mistakes</h2>

      <ol>
        <li>Using a demand letter where the statutory preliminary 20-day notice is required.</li>
        <li>Sending the notice only to the hiring party while omitting the owner, original contractor, or lender.</li>
        <li>Failing to send a supplemental notice after the estimated price increases by at least 30%.</li>
        <li>Counting 120 days from the claimant's last work instead of statutory project completion.</li>
        <li>Missing a recorded Notice of Completion that shortens the period to 60 days.</li>
        <li>Claiming early work not protected by a late preliminary notice.</li>
        <li>Omitting the preliminary notice or proof of mailing from the recorded claim.</li>
        <li>Waiting until the six-month foreclosure deadline to find counsel.</li>
      </ol>

