          <summary class="flex items-center justify-between p-5 cursor-pointer text-navy-800 font-medium hover:bg-slate-50 list-none">
            Can I serve the preliminary notice before starting work?
            <span class="text-slate-400 ml-4 shrink-0 group-open:rotate-180 transition-transform">▾</span>
          </summary>
          <div class="px-5 pb-5 text-slate-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">Yes. The statutory notice can be served before furnishing begins, which avoids a rushed calculation after mobilization.</div>
        </details>
        <details class="bg-white border border-slate-200 rounded-xl overflow-hidden group">
          <summary class="flex items-center justify-between p-5 cursor-pointer text-navy-800 font-medium hover:bg-slate-50 list-none">
            When does the 60-day deadline replace the 120-day deadline?
            <span class="text-slate-400 ml-4 shrink-0 group-open:rotate-180 transition-transform">▾</span>
          </summary>
          <div class="px-5 pb-5 text-slate-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">The 60-day period generally applies after the owner records a Notice of Completion. Without that recorded notice, the ordinary period is 120 days after statutory completion.</div>
        </details>
        <details class="bg-white border border-slate-200 rounded-xl overflow-hidden group">
          <summary class="flex items-center justify-between p-5 cursor-pointer text-navy-800 font-medium hover:bg-slate-50 list-none">
            Where is the lien recorded?
            <span class="text-slate-400 ml-4 shrink-0 group-open:rotate-180 transition-transform">▾</span>
          </summary>
          <div class="px-5 pb-5 text-slate-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">With the county recorder in the Arizona county where the property, or part of it, is located.</div>
        </details>
        <details class="bg-white border border-slate-200 rounded-xl overflow-hidden group">
          <summary class="flex items-center justify-between p-5 cursor-pointer text-navy-800 font-medium hover:bg-slate-50 list-none">
            How long do I have to enforce it?
            <span class="text-slate-400 ml-4 shrink-0 group-open:rotate-180 transition-transform">▾</span>
          </summary>
          <div class="px-5 pb-5 text-slate-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">Arizona's general foreclosure period is six months after the lien is recorded. Filing the lien does not pause that deadline while the parties negotiate.</div>
        </details>
      </div>

      <h2>Build a defensible claim file</h2>

      <p>Keep the contract, license information, change orders, invoices, delivery tickets, daily reports, payment ledger, preliminary notice, mailing proof, supplemental notices, owner and lender research, permit/final inspection records, Notice of Completion search, deed, legal description, and recorded lien copy together.</p>

      <p>After cleared payment, use the correct <a href="/mechanics-lien/release/">mechanics lien release</a>. If payment is being exchanged for a waiver, confirm whether a <a href="/lien-waiver/conditional/">conditional lien waiver</a> is safer until funds clear.</p>

      <p><em>This article provides general information, not legal advice. Arizona's notice, licensing, dwelling, completion, and enforcement rules are fact-sensitive. Verify current statutes or consult an Arizona construction attorney.</em></p>

